From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: EDEN_UP_PRESSURE_DRAIN=1|2: while the pool is short or memory was refused, the recording thread submits, waits up to 80 ms for the GPU and destroys the deleted buffers and images at once; off by default

Switches: EDEN_UP_PRESSURE_DRAIN=1 (frame ends, and inside a frame at the start of a draw or
dispatch, every 256th, at most every 50 ms), =2 (frame ends only), EDEN_UP_PRESSURE_DRAIN_COUNT=1
(count only: no flush, no wait, nothing freed). Numbers (digits, else the default):
_MIB=<MiB> pool short under this largest free piece (256), _PENDING_MIB=<MiB> deleted buffer
memory that makes a short pool worth a drain (64), _MS=<ms> longest wait (80, at most 1000),
_GAP_MS=<ms> shortest time between drains (500; a quarter of it after a refusal).
Lines (stderr): "EDEN_UP_PRESSURE_DRAIN mode ..." once; "EDEN_UP_PRESSURE_DRAIN <n> ..." for the
first 16 drains and every 64th; "EDEN_UP_PRESSURE_DRAIN_10S ..." every 10 s when anything was
looked at: drains, MiB freed, ms waited, waits that ran out, skips by reason, the deleted buffers
waiting (peak and now), the pool's largest free piece (low and now), the allocator's use.

Why (READ, a hand-played session, T151, the load into the area past the
field): at the refusal the allocator's device-local type held 3082 allocations, 3343 MiB
(EDEN_REFUSED_VMA type 0), while the texture cache held 1625 images, 1876 MiB, and the buffer cache
453 live buffers, 429 MiB: about 1000 allocations, about 1040 MiB, that no report names, about
1 MiB each (live buffers average 0.95 MiB). Census n=51 (fresh, the burst frame): retiring=0 (no
deleted image waiting), staging 32, decode 1, vma_slack 96, yet 1245 MiB of vma_used unnamed.
Docked (a hand-played session, the load): 2653 allocations, 2651 MiB, against 1339 images 1476 MiB and
470 buffers 365 MiB: about 840 allocations, 810 MiB, unnamed. Before the load (docked, line 4218)
the same sum leaves 78 allocations, 167 MiB.
The one holder that fits: the buffer cache's delayed_destruction_ring. A deleted buffer (every
buffer that grows leaves its old copy there, and the port's buffer collector runs harder while
memory is short) keeps its memory until 8 frame ends have passed; it is in no census field
(MemoryCensus::Buffers counts registered buffers, Retiring counts images only). A load frame takes
seconds (census gpu=stale for 8.5 s handheld, 48 s docked), so nothing in it goes back during the
load; earlier loads' "626-894 MiB unnamed, drained within 3 s" is the ring emptying 8 frames later.
(INFERRED from the counts; 0802's line and 0731's map would name it.) waveydude saw the same at a
later area's gate: GPU-only buffers 219 MiB in 289 buffers to 1,464 MiB in 1,626 within 2 s
(T153 0698's header).

What: RasterizerVulkan::PressureDrainLook at the end of TickFrame and at the top of PrepareDraw and
DispatchCompute (before FlushWork, so no draw is half set up). It reads the pool's largest free
piece (MemoryCensus::FreeLargest), the allocator's refusal counter, the buffer cache's refused
bytes, and walks the ring. With the pool short and _PENDING_MIB waiting, or after a refusal, at
most every _GAP_MS (a refused buffer counts once per frame: RefusedBytes stays set until the
frame's end, and the drain after it already let the cache ask again): both caches locked, scheduler.Flush(), MasterSemaphore::WaitFor(the tick
recorded so far, _MS). Reached: TextureCache::ReleaseFinished (deleted images the GPU is past),
BufferCache::ReleaseFinished (the whole ring), BufferCache::RetryRefusedNow, and the port's
StagingBufferPool::ReleaseCompleted where it exists. Not reached: only the images the GPU is past
and the staging it is done with; the ring stays, and no drain waits again until the GPU passes
that tick (Refresh, no wait), so a hung GPU still reaches RADV's 10 s no-progress check.
This is RasterizerVulkan::ReleaseFinishedMemory's work (EDEN_NX2_BUFFER_WAIT's relief after a
refused buffer, on by default) with a bounded wait, before a refusal instead of after it.

Safety: everything in the ring and in gpu_sentenced_images was deleted before this point, and at
these points every command that used it is recorded in the current tick or an earlier one; the
ring goes only once the GPU is past the current tick. No live image or buffer is deleted. A delta
draw (EDEN_UP_FAST_DRAW) resolves in full after a drain (Nx2FastDraw::Break, as at a frame end).
While the guest's conditional rendering is set nothing drains (its predicate buffer may be in the
ring; ReleaseFinishedMemory ends conditional rendering instead, which draws what the guest meant to
skip). The allocation that was refused has already taken its fallback (host memory, unbacked, or
the refused-buffer path); buffers are asked for again at once after a drain, images are not
re-made.
Price: a drain is a submit and a wait of up to 80 ms on the GPU thread (a hitch), at most every
500 ms. In the field the largest free piece sits under 192 MiB all the time, so _PENDING_MIB is
what keeps it from draining in plain play: the 10 s line says how often it drains.
Off: one static test per draw, per dispatch and per frame. Not in the shader stamp list.

